\par\par \b \uc2  Thai  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  says has not supported baht. \b0\par\par\uc2 183 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 1999\par 07:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, Oct 27 (Reuters) - Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Chatu Mongol Sonakul said on Wednesday the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the baht in recent days. \par \par \uc2  The governor told reporters the baht's recovery, to about 38.60 to the dollar on Wednesday from 39.75 on October 11 and a 13-month low of about 41.70 in late September, had probably been helped by improved exports last month. \par \par \uc2  "On the baht strength, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still has not done anything. We have seen some foreign capital inflow as September exports increased and some people have unwound their (dollar) positions," he said. \par \par \uc2  Chatu Mongol said a weaker baht last month had obviously boosted Thai exports. \par \par \uc2  Analysts expect Thai September exports to rise to about $5.0 billion, a 23-month high, from a preliminary $4.86 billion in August and $4.47 billion in September last year. \par \par \uc2  ((Vithoon Amorn, Bangkok newsroom +66-2 637 5500 ext 412 \par \par \uc2  email: bangkok.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dvar015mz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
