\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Ctrl Bk Buys Dlrs To Pay Off Swap Obligations-Govt \b0\par\par\uc2 313 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 1999\par 14:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  told the government Monday that it had bought dollars on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets only to settle swap market obligations, the government's spokesman said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Akapol Sorasuchart told reporters that the Bank of Thailand report said authorities are monitoring market movements very closely, but that the baht's recent weakness wasn't the reason for any of its actions to date. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy Governor Tanya Sirivedhin said late Monday that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market Friday to stem excessive volatility, when the dollar rose to a 13-month high of THB41.41. \par \par \uc2  She said a range of THB41 to THB42 to the dollar was acceptable to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but that it might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the baht became "unacceptably weak." She wouldn't be more specific. \par \par \uc2  The dollar moved up sharply again Tuesday to a new 13-month high of THB41.49, but fell back to an Asian close of THB41.335. \par \par \uc2  No signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were observed Tuesday, but authorities were contacting parties who submitted unusual bids or offers and asking for explanations, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the dollar to peak this year around THB42 to THB42.50, before inflows from short-term equity investment funds resume in the first quarter of next year. \par \par \uc2  The current baht weakness was sparked by expectations of a Chinese yuan devaluation, instability in East Timor, concerns over the slow progress in restructuring Thailand's non-performing loans, and the strength of the yen. The U.S. dollar's and yen's gains sparked a rush by local debtors and importers to hedge against future foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  obligations, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  -By Thomas Fox; 662 266 0744; tom.fox@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 28-09-99 \par \par \uc2  1212GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9s06yi1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
