\par\par \b \uc2  Baht seen falling further as debtors seek cover. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Anchalee Koetsawang \par \uc2 660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 1999\par 11:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, Sept 28 (Reuters) - The weak Thai baht is set to slide further near-term as corporate debtors, worried about yen strength, run for shelter. \par \par \uc2  Analysts believe that the baht's current sharp weakness is more due to poor sentiment and technical reasons rather than fundamental factors which have not changed much. \par \par \uc2  At 0745 GMT, the baht dropped to its lowest level since August 31, 1998 at 41.27/41.32 per dollar. It hovered around the 37 level in July but really started tumbling in September. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the baht could test the 41.50-42.00 level in the coming weeks unless there was strong indication the yen would weaken further or other strong positive leads emerged. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's veiled threat that it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the baht went too low might not be forceful enough to avert its imminent fall, but it could slow the pace, they said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past prided itself on telling the market that it would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should move with market forces. \par \par \uc2  "They (The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) are not telling you they are not worried any more. But this does not mean that the market will not try (to sell the baht), but they will be cautious," said Alison Seng, a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  specialist at Standard and Poors' MMS. \par \par \uc2  "The impact of the (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) statement was slightly diluted by an earlier statement that the baht at 41-42 level is acceptable. This is like an invitation for the market to go short," she said. \par \par \uc2  Recent baht weakness was caused mostly by corporate debt repayments on fears the yen could rise further, hedging activity, dollar demand for year-end book closings, and position adjustments on Y2K computer bug worries for the year-end. \par \par \uc2  "Our positive view on Thailand's economic fundamentals has not changed. Debt restructuring has improved and the export sector and consequently the trade surplus remains strong," said Chan Chia Lin, head of economic research at ABN AMRO Bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "The weakness we see now in the baht is caused by external and domestic factors. One major factor the market was talking about is debt repayment for yen-denominated loans," she said. \par \par \uc2  The Thai private sector's foreign debt as at the end of June was estimated at $45 billion, about 20 percent of which is believed to be yen-denominated. \par \par \uc2  The yen rose sharply recently, sparking speculation that it could test the key 100-yen level. Although it has calmed down a little after the latest G7 meeting, concerns still linger. \par \par \uc2  "Some of our corporate clients sold baht for yen to repay the debt, fearing that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could rise further and affect their debt servicing ability," said a senior dealer at a major U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  "At the same time, more companies hedged their positions in the forward market to minimise risks. This also pressured the baht as banks which sold forward contracts have to buy dollars in the spot market. The poor sentiment seems to feed on itself." \par \par \uc2  The problem was exacerbated by exporters' withholding of dollars on anticipation that the baht might drop further. \par \par \uc2  Treasurers at several major exporting firms surveyed by Reuters said they were in no rush to sell dollars. \par \par \uc2  "In this kind of market, we would rather hold on to the dollar as long as it is permitted by the government," said a corporate treasurer at a major agri-business company. \par \par \uc2  The government gives exporters 120 days to repatriate their export revenues to Thailand and seven days to change it into local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after that. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the baht was expected to climb again from current low levels in the medium-term with Thailand's trade surplus and other positive domestic fundamentals, but it might not see the 38 per dollar level again for the rest of the year. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9s02zys\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
