\par\par \b \uc2  EMERGING MARKET  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia up, Colombia set to drop. \b0\par\par\uc2 965 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 1999\par 13:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 27 (Reuters) - Emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed on Monday as players sold dollars after taking a closer look at comments on the yen from the Group of Seven (G7) industrial powers. \par \par \uc2  Colombia's newly floated peso was expected to tumble on the first trading day after the scrapping of its old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band, while the surging world gold price helped lift the South African rand sharply higher. \par \par \uc2  A weekend G7 statement expressed concern over the potential impact of the strong yen on the global economy, and triggered rumours the Bank of Japan might agree to ease credit in return for international cooperation to halt its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's climb. \par \par \uc2  The dollar reached a day's high of 106 yen on that view, but on closer scrutiny players decided the G7 statement contained little concrete sign of action on the yen and the dollar slipped back to around 104. \par \par \uc2  "There was no agreement on coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or policy change," said Callum Henderson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in London. \par \par \uc2  "Reassessment of that statement caused dollar/yen to fall and that provided a perfect excuse for profit-taking on long dollar positions in a number of exchange rates." \par \par \uc2  The volatile Indonesian rupiah showed the sharpest move against the greenback, strengthening past 8,400 in thin trade after grazing 8,900 late last week during clashes between students and security forces. \par \par \uc2  Jakarta was quiet after President Habibie delayed signing the controversial new security law that sparked the riots. But analysts said the calm was unlikely to last as student groups have vowed to continue their protest until the law is scrapped. \par \par \uc2  They said the rupiah's stronger tone owed more to last week's tough parliamentary report saying seven top officials, including the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor and the finance minister, should face swift legal action over the Bank Bali scandal. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht, which has led regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness over the past couple of weeks, firmed back through the key barrier of 41 to the dollar, helped by rumours in Singapore that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the forward market late last week. \par \par \uc2  But analysts saw further medium-term weakness as a heavy burden of non-performing loans continued to weigh on the Thai banking sector and players nervous of the millennium bug became increasingly wary of emerging Asian assets. \par \par \uc2  "Medium-term technicals point to 43.50 in perhaps a six-month frame," Henderson at Citibank said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar and Philippine peso were also significantly firmer. \par \par \uc2  COLOMBIA PESO'S SLIDE SEEN LIMITED \par \par \uc2  Colombia's peso was expected to slip when American markets opened later on Monday, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  scrapped its trading band and crawling peg system on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  But most analysts said the slide should be limited as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not drastically overvalued, and was likely to bounce back towards current levels later in the week. \par \par \uc2  The peso closed on Friday at 1,994.49 to the dollar, and most analysts saw it tumbling on Monday to between 2,000 and 2,200. \par \par \uc2  "It depends how the Americans view it, but it won't be there for long - it'll come back a bit towards the end of the week," said Mike Noone, Latin America economist at Westdeutsche Landesbank in London. \par \par \uc2  The move to a free float followed persistent market attacks on the band, and was announced one day after Colombia clinched an agreement with the International Monetary Fund for a $7 billion international loan package. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had haemorrhaged more than $400 million in reserves last week alone to try to defend the band. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the rand firmed to hover around 6.0 to the dollar after world gold prices soared on Sunday's news that 15 European central banks had agreed to cap gold sales. The metal was fixed in London at $281.70 a troy ounce, its highest since early May, against Friday afternoon's $270. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the rise had a strong emotional impact on the spot rand market, although gold accounts for less than one-fifth of South Africa's export earnings. \par \par \uc2  Comments by Reserve Bank Governor Tito Mboweni on the need for caution on further monetary easing after last week's prime rate cut also supported the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts added. ((Gill Tudor, London Capital Markets +44 171 542 6414, fax 583 7239, gill.tudor@reuters.com)). \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab \tab     LATEST  PREV    PCT CHG  PCT CHG\par CLOSE\tab \tab in 1999\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPE/MEAST/AFRICA\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Czech Rep.\tab \tab     35.86   35.93   -0.19%   3.05%\par Poland\tab \tab \tab    4.27    4.27   -0.12%   4.89%\par Hungary\tab \tab \tab 255.32  255.55   -0.09%   1.64%\par Slovakia\tab \tab \tab 43.16   43.00    0.38%   0.38%\par Romania\tab \tab \tab 16,410  16,385    0.15%  49.86%\par Ukraine\tab \tab \tab   4.50    4.49    0.22%  14.50%\par South Africa\tab \tab    6.00    6.04   -0.55%   2.69%\par Kenya\