\par\par \b \uc2  Fin Min To Explain Thai Recovery At World Bank/IMF Meet \b0\par\par\uc2 367 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 1999\par 14:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--Thai Finance Minister Tarrin Nimmanahaeminda said he will explain at the annual International Monetary Fund and World Bank meeting how the Thai government recovered to economic growth forecast at 3% to 4% this year, from a contraction of 10% last year. \par \par \uc2  Prior to his departure later Wednesday, Tarrin also told reporters that the meeting in Washington will focus on how to help countries which can't help themselves. \par \par \uc2  The minister refused to comment on the baht, which hit another 12-month low of THB40.44 to the dollar before recovering slightly at the Asian close, other than to say it was the result of market forces. No abnormal speculative activity has been spotted by the authorities, he said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to damp volatility or to interrupt speculative activity, not to counter market trends. \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts have said there is high demand for dollars and yen from local corporations with foreign debts, and importers with upcoming obligations. Servicing foreign debt gets more expensive as the baht weakens, especially debt denominated in yen, as the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been strengthening. \par \par \uc2  In wide-ranging but brief comments, Tarrin said low growth in outstanding commercial bank credit was slightly misleading because corporations are turning more to the domestic bond market. He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs data from the Securities & Exchange Commission so that combined figures can be compared. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Thailand said Tuesday it will lower its 1999 commercial bank lending growth target of 4% to 7%, as it is apparent it won't be met. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't say what the new target would be. \par \par \uc2  Tarrin said the baht value of outstanding credit also fluctuates with the exchange rate, because the total includes foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated loans from domestic branches with Bangkok International Banking Facilities, or BIBF, licences. When the baht strengthens, total lending appears to drop. For accurate comparison of lending at different periods, the total figure should be adjusted, he said. \par \par \uc2  -By Thomas Fox; 662 266 0744; tom.fox@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 22-09-99 \par \par \uc2  1231GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9m06yad\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
