\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED-Thailand does not favour strong baht-finmin Tarrin. \b0\par\par\uc2 594 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 1999\par 11:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  In Sept 7 BANGKOK story headlined "Thailand does not favour strong baht-finmin Tarrin"...please read in second paragraph... "On a permanent basis to keep the baht unrealistically weak is not a correct policy because we would sell cheap and buy expensive in baht terms"...instead of "We will try to primarily keep the baht weaker in order to help exporters and as it could be a factor contributing to the (Thai) economic recovery"...(correcting quote). \par \par \uc2  A corrected repetition follows: \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, Sept 7 (Reuters) - Thai authorities are not in favour of a strong baht because a firmer \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have a negative impact, Finance Minister Tarrin Nimmanahaeminda said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "On a permanent basis to keep the baht unrealistically weak is not a correct policy because we would sell cheap and buy expensive in baht terms," he told a social club gathering. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, he added: "The government has no policy to make the baht unrealistically strong." \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Tuesday the baht tumbled past the 39 baht per dollar level to its lowest since October 5 1998. \par \par \uc2  The baht was quoted sharply lower late on Tuesday at 39.15-39.20 after opening the day near 38.75-38.80 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the baht slid along with other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors chilled by mayhem in East Timor switched to dollars from regional Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the Indonesian rupiah. \par \par \uc2  There were no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the baht's fall, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Tarrin also that massive non-performing loans (NPLs) carried by Thai financial institutions and recapitalisation of banks were the major economic problems faced by Thailand which sank into recession in mid-1997 but has lately shown few signs of recovery. \par \par \uc2  Latest data available show that Thai banks carry about 2.7 trillion baht or 47 percent of total lending. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  classifies loans on which interest repayments have not been made for three months as NPLs. \par \par \uc2  "Now that everything has been stabilised I see the NPLs problems and the question of helping commercial banks raise sufficient capital as remaining major problems that require sustained solutions," he said. \par \par \uc2  "The NPL problem in the business sectors and insufficient capital of banks are two sides of the same coin," he added. \par \par \uc2  The Thai authorities had since last August to now helped banks raise their capital by about 600 billion baht, he said. \par \par \uc2  By doing this, the authorities had almost doubled the capital of local banks which in their whole history had built up only 700 billion baht in capital, he added. \par \par \uc2  Tarrin said that if NPLs could be reduced to around 25 percent of total bank lending from 47 percent now, they would reach appropriate levels conducive to sustainable Thai economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "NPLs should be looked at in baht terms not in percentage terms. NPLs have stabilised in baht terms. In June they declined by 70 billion baht and we are waiting for the July figure," he said. \par \par \uc2  The minister said there had been progress in NPLs restructuring and to date a total of 690 billion baht in NPLs had been restructured by banks. \par \par \uc2  He expected that by the year-end this could reach one trillion baht. \par \par \uc2  On NPLs classification, he said: "The Bank of Thailand standards do not themselves cause NPLs although they may add burdens (causing provisioning burdens for commercial banks)." \par \par \uc2  "It would be a dream to believe that easing these standards would help solve the problem," he added. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv98013e1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
