\par\par \b \uc2  Thai baht firmer on profit-taking offshore. \b0\par\par\uc2 350 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 1999\par 10:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0708 GMT - Thai baht\tab   strengthens in afternoon trade on Wednesday as foreign players start to sell dollars to take profit\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Baht 39.275/39.325 per dlr vs 39.32/39.35 early Wednesday\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  * Baht 41.7415/41.8143 per euro vs 41.6553/41.7264 \par \par \uc2  * Y 110.90/110.96 per dlr vs 111.01/111.06 \par \par \uc2  * One-month forward swap discounts quoted at -10.50/-9.50 satangs per dlr onshore; 0.00/2.00 offshore \par \par \uc2  * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's one-day repurchase rate 0.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, Sept 8 (Reuters) - The baht was firmer in afternoon trade on Wednesday as foreign players sold the dollar to take profits. \par \par \uc2  The move followed overbuying of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the European and New York markets on Tuesday, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  "There was a market correction after the baht swung to a very low level," said a dealer at a local bank. \par \par \uc2  At 0721 GMT, the baht was trading at 39.30/35 having hit an intraday low of 39.43, its weakest level since September 30. \par \par \uc2  Traders identified resistance at 39.30 and support at 39.40. \par \par \uc2  "Trade in the afternoon could be volatile," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they had not seen any sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Top bankers said on Wednesday the baht situation over the past two days would be "only temporary" and that the government should not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  the market. \par \par \uc2  They said a baht at 38 to 40 to the dollar would benefit exports. \par \par \uc2  "The baht is not likely to fall further," said Chavalit Thanachanan, chairman of Bank of Asia ABN and former governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Nimit Nontapunthawat, chief economist at Bangkok Bank, said Thailand's economic fundamentals remained sound, pointing to the country's current account surplus average of about $1 billion each month. \par \par \uc2  "Low interest rates reduce motives for speculating in the baht," he said. "It will soon regain momentum." \par \par \uc2  ((Ekarin Petsiri +66-2 637 5500 ext 417 \par \par \uc2  email:bangkok.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv98012gt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
