\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Dep Fin Min: Baht Volatile On Strong Yen, Politics \b0\par\par\uc2 299 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1999\par 08:52\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--Thai Deputy Finance Minister Pisit Leeahtam said Friday the baht's recent volatility is due to a combination of external and internal factors, notably the strength of the yen and domestic political uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has stabilized in a range of THB38.70 to THB38.75 Friday, after surging from less than THB38.40 Wednesday and briefly touching an 11-month high of THB38.93 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Pisit told reporters that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets this week because the volatility wasn't too great. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Chatu Mongol Sonakul said earlier there was no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because the movement wasn't caused by speculative activity. \par \par \uc2  Pisit said the strength of the yen, currently below Y110 against the U.S. dollar, from above Y120 a few weeks ago, is causing global money markets to be highly sensitive. \par \par \uc2  Officials said earlier that the run of the dollar from below THB38.40 this week was triggered by repatriation of funds by Japanese and other foreign companies in Thailand, and dollar short-covering by some U.S. funds. \par \par \uc2  Pisit said political uncertainties also contributed to baht volatility this week. \par \par \uc2  It has been speculated that there will be a new general election within the next 12 months, before the current government under the premiership of Chuan Leekpai completes its four-year term in November 2000. \par \par \uc2  Most analysts believe it is unlikely that Chuan will call an election until the second quarter next year, and that coalition partners of his Democrat party will stick with the government to take part of the credit for an improving economy. \par \par \uc2  -By Chanyaporn Chanjaroen; 662-266-0744; cchanjaroen@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 03-09-99 \par \par \uc2  0652GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9306iiy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
