\par\par \b \uc2  Thai c. bank says no plan to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  for baht. \b0\par\par\uc2 293 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 1999\par 14:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, June 28 (Reuters) - Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Chatu Mongol Sonakul reaffirmed on Monday a policy of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to influence the baht's exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  Chatu Mongol told reporters he believed the baht's current rate, at about 36.60-36.80 to the dollar, was backed by sound fundamentals. He did not elaborate. \par \par \uc2  "I believe the baht, at its current rate, is based on right fundamentals...and on the right track...I don't think it is too strong and definitely not hurting exports," the governor said. \par \par \uc2  The baht rallied to its five-month high of about 36.55 to the dollar last Tuesday and had since hovered in the 36.60-36.80 range compared with 37.00-38.00 in recent months. \par \par \uc2  The baht was quoted at 36.83/36.93 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Deputy Prime Minister Supachai Panitchpakdi said last week the baht's strength could hurt exports if its rallies were not checked. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said his bank maintained its normal monitoring of the baht and any of its entries into the market was for meeting its normal dollar/baht swap obligations. \par \par \uc2  "The baht's gains simply reflect confidence of investors," he said. \par \par \uc2  Chatu Mongol said the Thai authorities had no plan to do to the baht what the Japanese government had done to try to slow the yen's appreciation. The Bank of Japan has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets several times this month to curb the yen's rise following strong first quarter GDP data. \par \par \uc2  ((Vithoon Amorn, Bangkok newsroom (662)-6375500 ext 412 \par \par \uc2  e-mail: bangkok.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6s05iio\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
