\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Baht Ends Flat; Mkt Awaits Key U.S. Data \b0\par\par\uc2 458 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 1999\par 12:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai baht ended flat against the U.S. dollar in Asian spot trading Wednesday, in a subdued market ahead of the release of key data on the U.S. inflation rate later in the global session. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 36.95 baht (THB), only marginally changed from THB36.94 late Tuesday in Asia. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved in a very tight range of TH36.94-THB36.97 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  A similarly tight range of THB36.92 to THB36.98 could be expected Thursday, unless the dollar finds new direction after the release of the May consumer price index overnight. \par \par \uc2  Markets expect U.S. interest rates to rise, and the U.S. inflation rate is considered a leading indicator of any action that might be taken by the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee to boost rates at the end of the month. \par \par \uc2  A trade surplus and investment inflows support the baht, but foreign debt payments and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying on weakness for reserves keep a floor under the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The baht should stay in a very tight range, but I think the dollar will still be testing the downside," in line with its recent trend against the yen, a trader at a foreign bank said. But the Bank of Japan halted the dollar's decline earlier this week with forceful \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  set its reference rate at THB36.978 Wednesday, compared with THB36.954 Tuesday. The rate is a weighted average of rates charged by Thai commercial banks the previous trading day and serves as a benchmark for domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading. \par \par \uc2  Swap and forward premiums are flat to slightly higher, compared with levels recorded late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  An increase in swap and forward rates generally indicates greater demand for baht - used either to set up long-dollar positions that would profit from a future depreciation of the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or to cover short-baht positions. Fluctuations in supply can also affect rates. \par \par \uc2  Offshore swap premiums, in satang, were as follows (THB1=100 satang): \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par          Wednesday   Tuesday    Monday      Friday\par 1-mo       1.0         1.0      -1.5/-0.5   -1.5/-0.5\par 2-mo     1.0/2.5     0.5/1.5    -1.0/1.0    -1.0/1.0\par 3-mo     2.5/3.5     2.5/3.5     1.0/2.5       1.5\par 6-mo    10.0/12.0    8.5/10.5    7.0/10.0    7.0/9.0\par 1-yr    24.0/28.0   23.0/27.0   18.0/23.0   18.0-23.0\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Thomas Fox; 662 266 0744; tfox@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 16-06-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1033GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1028 1861 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6g04m6j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
