\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Asia: Tearaway Baht Poses Awkward Policy Dilemma \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 908 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 1999\par 09:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Two years after the Bank of Thailand slapped controls on its capital account in an ultimately doomed attempt to avert the collapse of the baht, monetary authorities in Bangkok are facing a problem they could barely have imagined just 24 months ago. The baht is strengthening rapidly, and short of imposing fresh capital controls there is little the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or anybody else can do to stop it. \par \par \uc2  Before profit-taking set in on Thursday afternoon and trimmed its gains, the baht was trading at a 16-week high. With the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at THB36.76, the baht was an impressive 54% above its all time low of THB56.75 to dollar, hit in January 1998. \par \par \uc2  The baht's rally has been fueled by a combination of trade flows and an inrush of foreign funds destined for investment in Bangkok's soaring stock market. \par \par \uc2  Although down from a year earlier, Thailand's trade balance was still firmly in surplus over the first quarter at $1.73 billion - a residual effect of the collapse in imports which followed the baht's devaluation in 1997 and helped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the way to recovery. \par \par \uc2  Imports may now be rising, but economists interpret the narrowing of the surplus positively, saying greater demand for imports is a crucial sign of economic recovery. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Economy Emerging From Recession\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Thailand is clearly coming out of recession," says Neil Saker,\par regional economist at SG Securities in Singapore. "Production is picking\par up, banks are restructuring and recapitalizing, and bankers are prepared\par to lend to good companies."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Other foreign analysts agree.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Thailand's economic contraction was over by the end of the second\par quarter of 1998. By the fourth quarter, the economy was growing again,"\par says Adrian Foster, South East Asian economist at Nomura International\par in Hong Kong, forecasting economic growth of 2.5% for this year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The message has not been lost on investors. The decisions this week by\par both Moody's Investor Service and Standard & Poor's to lift their\par outlook for the Kingdom's sovereign credit rating merely confirmed the\par belief of many that Thailand, the first Asian country to descend into\par crisis, would be among the first to emerge.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Smoothing the path to recovery have been massive monetary easings\par which have seen baht interest rates collapse to levels close to U.S.\par dollar rates. Faced with dismal returns on deposits, Thai savers have\par turned instead to the local stock market, driving the Stock Exchange of\par Thailand's benchmark index up by 40% since the beginning of April.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Foreign investors have not been slow to catch the trend. Over recent\par weeks offshore fund managers have been pouring money into the Thailand\par at an ever accelerating pace, driving the baht sharply higher.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And according to some analysts, there is no obvious reason why the\par baht's appreciation should stop in the near term. According to Foster at\par Nomura, one of the major risks that has so far deterred foreign\par investors - the high proportion of non-performing loans in the banking\par sector - may have been greatly exaggerated.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par According to the Bank of Thailand, 47% of all loans were\par non-performing at the end of February. Foster, however, says the real\par picture may be far less serious. A large proportion of the loans\par classified as non-performing Foster describes as "strategic NPLs" which\par borrowers have stopped servicing simply because the opportunity arose,\par even if they are in fact capable of making payments.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Bad Loan Levels Seen Dropping Rapidly\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "To think that half a country's bank loans are bad is inconceivable,"\par he says, arguing that with the erection of a sounder legal framework,\par payments on many loans will resume and the level of NPLs will fall off\par rapidly.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Less bad debt will mean more lending, brisker economic activity,\par higher credit ratings, and more inward investment driving the baht\par higher, say economists. And despite protests from Thai exporters, there\par is little the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can do to slow the appreciation.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be one potential option, but buying dollars would\par inject even more liquidity into the domestic market, further fueling the\par stock market rally and attracting more foreign funds, adding to the\par upward pressure on the baht.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In any case, say \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  watchers, resistance to direct\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains high within the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , after a series of\par futile actions to defend the baht bought the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to its knees\par two years ago.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "What we are seeing now is a 180 degree turn around in the policy\par dilemma - a complete reversal," says Saker at SG.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The authorities could attempt to slow the baht's rise by tightening\par either fiscal or monetary policy to take some of the steam out of the\par stock market, but either course would put upward pressure on interest\par rates, something policy-makers are keen to avoid.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There is a feeling that if they raise interest rates they risk\par sucking in hot money," says Saker, "but lower interest rates will suck\par money into the stock market."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The only answer, not only for Thailand but for other Asian countries\par facing a similar dilemma, suggests Saker, may well prove to be capital\par controls modeled on those adopted in Chile, which permitted longer term\par investment, while penalizing short term capital flows.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Tom Holland; 65 421 4800\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par tom.holland@cor.dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 07-05-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0712GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv5703g7n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
