\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Asia: Regional  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Seen Nearing Top Of Bull Run \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 939 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 1999\par 07:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are unlikely to keep pace with an extended run-up in the yen, as market players fret that local central banks will step in to brake, or block entirely, any further appreciation. \par \par \uc2  A sell-off in the U.S. dollar against the yen overnight Tuesday and early Wednesday in Asia drove a sharp rally in the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Singapore dollar, for example, gained 1.5% against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as offshore players were forced out of their long U.S. dollar positions by the yen-induced rise of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But although dealers in Asia expect renewed selling to drag the U.S. dollar as low as Y118 when European markets open later in the day, market participants believe that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is close to finding a bottom against its Asian regional counterparts. \par \par \uc2  "Regional central banks have made it clear that they don't want their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen, so you can expect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stand in the way," warns Ron Leven, Asian markets strategist at J.P. Morgan in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  As investor funds pouring into Asia in recent weeks have boosted local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , regional markets have seen a barrage of dollar-supportive verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  and finance ministry officials from around the region. In Taiwan and South Korea, the authorities have actually entered the local markets, buying U.S. dollars in an attempt to forestall the rise of the New Taiwan dollar and the won. \par \par \uc2  "Asian governments don't want their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen dramatically. Given that the recovery in regional economies is uncertain and by no means entrenched, they still want to have competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," explains Thio Chin Loo, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Banque Paribas in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Technical Analysts See Dollar Nearing Bottom \par \par \uc2  While fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a major factor, it is not the only reason traders and analysts believe the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are approaching the top of their run. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is fast approaching key technical support levels against the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which dealers think are extremely unlikely to be breached. \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar, many dealers are eying the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 200-day moving average at S$1.6925, a level they say should provide solid support for the U.S. dollar. And even if that is breached, they believe the market will certainly balk at pushing the U.S. dollar below its long term upward trend line at S$1.6870, given the slow pace of economic recovery the island is thought likely to see. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's floor against the Thai baht (THB) is more difficult to call, especially in the short term. With Thai markets closed for much of this week, trading in the baht is likely to be thin and whippy, warn dealers. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, Wednesday morning saw the U.S. dollar bottom out at THB37.5100, as traders shied away from pushing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down through the THB37.5000 level at which the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar in March. \par \par \uc2  If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declines to defend that level, however, traders and analysts agree that the authorities would have little hesitation in entering the market to buy U.S. dollars if the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatened to drop below the THB37.00 mark. \par \par \uc2  Similarly, market participants believe the U.S. dollar's potential losses against the Philippine peso are limited, after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped to a low of PHP38.060 on Wednesday morning from PHP38.295 at the close of trading the previous day. \par \par \uc2  "The Philippine government has clearly indicated that it is not comfortable with the peso stronger than PHP38.00" to the U.S. dollar, says Cliff Tan, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rate strategist at Warburg Dillon Read in Singapore. "The authorities don't want the external sector, which has been the economy's only piston of growth, to fall into recession." \par \par \uc2  North Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also have little scope to strengthen further from current levels over the short term, according to analysts and traders. \par \par \uc2  Although the South Korean won rose more than 0.5% Wednesday morning, with the U.S. dollar hitting a low of KRW1,215.00, dealers say the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to encounter solid support at levels between KRW1,200 to KRW1,210. \par \par \uc2  Government-directed buying of U.S. dollars by the state-run Export-Import Bank, coupled with forthcoming repayments of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt obligations, will draw a line under the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline at KRW1,200, agree analysts and traders. \par \par \uc2  In Taiwan, market participants believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to support the U.S. dollar at NT$37.75, and subsequently at NT$37.50, should fund inflows force a retreat from the first level. Beyond that, however, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be "very unhappy indeed" according to one trader. \par \par \uc2  Few analysts are attempting to pick levels for the dollar against the Indonesian rupiah (IDR), however. With the dollar barely straying beyond an IDR8,000 to IDR9,000 band this year, and little capital flowing either in or out of the country, there are no trends to whet the market's appetite. Although some market observers predict the rupiah may fall in advance of Indonesia's June 7 parliamentary elections, others argue that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves are more than sufficient to offset any likely outflows. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 65 421 4800; tom.holland@cor.dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 14-04-99 \par \par \uc2  0508GMT \par \par \uc2  4258 5095 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv4e02ss8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
