\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Finance Minister Reaffirms Market-Driven Baht Policy \b0\par\par\uc2 341 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 19:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--Thai Finance Minister Tarrin Nimmanhaeminda late Wednesday said that the country's financial authorities would continue to let the market set the exchange rate for the baht (THB) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Tarrin told delegates of a World Bank conference on Asian corporate recovery that an appropriate exchange rate had to consider more than just export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  The interests of importers and debtors also had to be taken into account, he said. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to stem volatility, not to fight market trends. \par \par \uc2  At the close of Asian spot trading Wednesday, the dollar was at THB37.64, down from THB37.68 late the previous day. \par \par \uc2  The government is ensuring that there is enough liquidity for exporters to continue operating, so there is no credit crunch in the sector, Tarrin said. \par \par \uc2  "We suffer more from lower prices" due to slack demand in global markets, he said. \par \par \uc2  Appropriate measures to improve competitiveness included improvements in product quality and production standards, mutual assistance within regional organizations, and development of trade with neighboring countries and southern China, he said. \par \par \uc2  "We will try our very best to achieve our 4% export growth target," Tarrin said. \par \par \uc2  Thailand is the eighth-largest exporter in the world, and the export sector accounts for 45% of gross domestic product. If the baht weakens a bit, it will account for 50% of GDP, Tarrin said. Export growth of 4% should result in GDP growth of about 1%, given that the import component of Thai exports is about 50%, he said. \par \par \uc2  The government target for 1999 is for 1% GDP growth. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Tuesday, the Bank of Thailand said export growth was behind the\par target. If the trend continues, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said, the government\par will need to take additional measures to stimulate the economy, beyond a\par THB130 billion package of tax cuts and spending programs announced the\par same day.\par    -By Thomas Fox; 662-266-0744; tfox@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 31-03-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1732GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv3v02hqd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
