\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA MARKETS - Boosted by firm yen, bullish Dow. \b0\par\par\uc2      By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 1,240 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 1999\par 10:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      SINGAPORE, Jan 11 (Reuters) - The strong yen kept most \par \par \uc2  Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a firm footing on Monday as the Dow's \par \par \uc2  105-point rise spurred regional stock markets to extend recent \par \par \uc2  gains. YEN RISE WORRIES REGIONAL CENTRAL BANKS \par \par \uc2      The dollar hovered below 111 yen in late Asian trade amid \par \par \uc2  growing pessimism after Friday's stronger-than-expected U.S. \par \par \uc2  jobs data failed to drive it convincingly higher. \par \par \uc2      The yen's resilience and corresponding rises in regional \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have sparked growing anxiety among Asian central \par \par \uc2  banks about a loss of export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2      The Bank of Korea stepped in to stem the won's rise last \par \par \uc2  week and Seoul dealers cited further signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \par \par \uc2  Monday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also believed to have bought \par \par \uc2  dollars against the baht in recent sessions. Manila \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  dealers are beginning to expect officials to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap \par \par \uc2  the peso's rise. \par \par \uc2      Collapsing regional imports have taken the steam out of \par \par \uc2  Asia's much-touted export-led recovery. Regional governments \par \par \uc2  now fear an appreciation in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  might hamper it \par \par \uc2  further. \par \par \uc2      KOREAN WON SPOOKED BUT SEEN SUPPORTED \par \par \uc2      The Korean won was one of the few regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \par \par \uc2  lose ground as two state-run banks actively bought dollars, \par \par \uc2  absorbing sales by exporters and foreigners investing in the \par \par \uc2  stock market. \par \par \uc2      The won fell to a low of 1,183 per dollar from a high of \par \par \uc2  1,150 after the Export-Import Bank of Korea ploughed in with \par \par \uc2  hefty dollar bids, trading directly in the local market for the \par \par \uc2  first time. \par \par \uc2      Analysts said Seoul's efforts to slow the won's rise could \par \par \uc2  succeed, but any falls would also be limited. \par \par \uc2      "The government has announced measures to remove the issue \par \par \uc2  of international bonds in 1999 and has asked state enterprises \par \par \uc2  not to borrow and start repaying some loans. So that will \par \par \uc2  reduce the supply of U.S. dollars and cap the strength of the \par \par \uc2  won," said Irene Cheung, regional economist at CIBC in \par \par \uc2  Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "But fundamentally, I think there is support for the \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and I don't expect it to weaken much below 1,200 per \par \par \uc2  dollar in the first half of 1999," Cheung told Reuters \par \par \uc2  Television. \par \par \uc2  KOREA RAISES 1999 GROWTH FORECAST \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Korea raised its 1999 GDP forecast to 3.2 \par \par \uc2  percent growth from a previous projection of one percent \par \par \uc2  growth, based largely on an expected surge in semiconductor \par \par \uc2  exports. \par \par \uc2      This compares with an expected contraction of five to six \par \par \uc2  percent for 1998. \par \par \uc2      The bullish outlook boosted the stock market, already up in \par \par \uc2  early trade on strong demand for newly listed Hanvit Bank. \par \par \uc2      Hanvit, the result of a merger between the Commercial Bank \par \par \uc2  of Korea and Hanil Bank, shot up to its daily limit high of \par \par \uc2  7,590 won at the opening bell. \par \par \uc2      PESO FIRMS BUT MARKET WARY \par \par \uc2      The Philippine peso rose in line with the yen, but the \par \par \uc2  37.50 per dollar level was expected to pose a stiff barrier. \par \par \uc2      Dealers were also wary after weekend press reports the \par \par \uc2  policy-making Monetary Board had permitted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \par \par \uc2  replenish its foreign reserves by buying dollars. \par \par \uc2      Manila stocks snapped an 11-day winning streak, but brokers \par \par \uc2  said any correction should be short-lived as interest in the \par \par \uc2  market remained strong. KL SHARES UPBEAT ON CABINET RESHUFFLE \par \par \uc2      Kuala Lumpur shares responded well to Prime Minister \par \par \uc2  Mahathir Mohamad's weekend cabinet reshuffle, which saw former \par \par \uc2  foreign minister Abdullah Ahmad Badawi named Deputy Prime \par \par \uc2  Minister and Daim Zainuddin appointed Finance Minister. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Abdullah's appointment reduced the likelihood \par \par \uc2  of a potentially damaging power struggle for the number two \par \par \uc2  post. \par \par \uc2      And the market-savvy Daim was expected to provide a steady \par \par \uc2  hand in charting Malaysia out of its recession. \par \par \uc2      The National Economic Action Council said concerns over the \par \par \uc2  government's ability to finance its 62 billion ringgit economic \par \par \uc2  recovery plan through 2000 were unfounded. \par \par \uc2      It said two-thirds of the funds would be sourced locally, \par \par \uc2  mainly through loans from the state pension fund and Petronas. \par \par \uc2      But data released on Monday showed the economy remained in \par \par \uc2  poor shape, with industrial output shrinking 11.5 percent \par \par \uc2  year-on-year in November after a 10.4 percent drop in October. \par \par \uc2      RUPIAH EDGES UP, \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  VOICES SUPPORT \par \par \uc2      The Indonesian rupiah was slightly firmer, but showed no \par \par \uc2  inclination to stray from its recent range near the 8,000 \par \par \uc2  level. \par \par \uc2      Dealers expected rising dollar demand ahead of the Moslem \par \par \uc2  Eid al-Fitr celebrations on January 19/20. \par \par \uc2      Bank Indonesia governor Syahril Sabirin said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2  bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was prepared to step in if necessary. \par \par \uc2      "We are trying our best to make it stable. If some \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary we will do so. We do not want to see \par \par \uc2  the rate fluctuate in a wider band," he told Reuters Television \par \par \uc2  in Hong Kong ahead of a central bankers' meeting. \par \par \uc2  Sabirin said there was room for interest rates to ease \par \par \uc2  further and the rupiah strengthen, but added its current level \par \par \uc2  was all right. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN, HONG KONG SHARES BUCK UPTREND \par \par \uc2  Most stock markets rode the Dow's gains, though Taipei \par \par \uc2  shares ended a shade softer on some late profit-taking. \par \par \uc2  Brokers saw stability in the near-term as the index has \par \par \uc2  held above the 10-year moving average at 6,300 for several \par \par \uc2  sessions. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong stocks ended weak after a choppy day, as \par \par \uc2  investors vacillated between Friday's interest rate cuts and \par \par \uc2  news foreign creditors would not receive priority treatment in \par \par \uc2  the bankruptcy of Guangdong International Trust and Investment \par \par \uc2  Corp (GITIC). \par \par \uc2      Change on the day \par \par \uc2   Change on