\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Baht Ends Higher On Late Local Buying In Quiet Market \b0\par\par\uc2 231 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 1998\par 13:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai baht (THB) ended higher in Asian trade Thursday, as late local buying broke into an otherwise quiet pre-holiday market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at THB37.71, down from THB37.83 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell almost to a support level at THB37.70 in late trade. A dealer at a Hong Kong bank in Bangkok said one of the big local banks sold a large quantity of dollars. She declined to name the bank. \par \par \uc2  Otherwise, trade was dominated by position-squaring ahead of a three-day weekend. Thailand's markets are closed Friday for a public holiday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar touched a morning high of THB37.90 on baht-selling by local banks, but the baht recovered to Wednesday's levels by midday as orders largely died out, according to a dealer at a French bank. \par \par \uc2  Dealers have said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bought and sold dollars in recent weeks to moderate the baht's ascent, but the Hong Kong bank dealer said there was no evidence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  participating in the market Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said in recent weeks that it isn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, but is buying and selling dollars to settle forward contracts. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 22-10-98 \par \par \uc2  1109GMT \par \par \uc2  1028 1861 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010917duam00dry\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\p